solution:
Sort the meetings by start time and sweep through them, keeping one busy block open at a time. A meeting that starts inside the block (or exactly at its end) stretches it; a meeting that starts later closes the block, adds its length to the total and opens a new one.

```js
function busyMinutes(meetings) {
  const sorted = [...meetings].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  let total = 0;
  let blockStart = 0;
  let blockEnd = -Infinity;
  for (const [start, end] of sorted) {
    if (start > blockEnd) {
      if (blockEnd > blockStart) total += blockEnd - blockStart;
      blockStart = start;
      blockEnd = end;
    } else {
      blockEnd = Math.max(blockEnd, end);
    }
  }
  if (blockEnd > blockStart) total += blockEnd - blockStart;
  return total;
}
```

One detail carries the tests: `Math.max` keeps a meeting nested inside the block, like `[20, 30]` inside `[0, 100]`, from pulling the end backwards. Sorting a copy (`[...meetings]`) is good practice rather than something the tests check: it leaves the caller's array untouched. Adding up each meeting's length without merging fails as soon as two meetings overlap, which is the whole point of the problem.
